      achievement_drive: [70, 0.55, "s", "D"],
      // [src_leibniz_sep, src_leibniz_mactutor, src_leibniz_merz1884 | 1672-1700 | scholarly reference + math biography + Victorian biography] SEP calls the 1672 Paris mission 'the single most important opportunity of his life' and records that he used it to meet Huygens and read Pascal's and Descartes's manuscripts; Merz records that conversations with Sophie Charlotte of Brandenburg over Bayle's writings led to the Theodicy and to the idea of an academy in Berlin, which Leibniz had prepared for years and took up when she showed interest. Two periods, secondary sources; scored moderately because the opportunities came partly through patrons.
